{"maxId":290,"entries":{"chapter-75-study-valvular-heart-disease|Chapter 75 Study: Valvular Heart Disease":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Prevalence 2–9% of older patients; leading clinically significant valvular disorder in older adults (p1149). 90% of AS in patients >65y is calcific degeneration of a tricuspid valve (p1149).":["u3"],"copy|Valve sclerosis (thickening, no obstruction): 25% of >65y, 48% of >75y; progresses to frank stenosis in ~10% within 5 years (p1150).":["u4"],"copy|Average progression: jet velocity +0.3 m/s/yr, valve area −0.1 cm²/yr (p1150).":["u5"],"copy|Severe high-gradient AS: peak velocity ≥4.0 m/s or mean gradient ≥40 mm Hg, with valve area typically ≤1.0 cm². Assess flow and measurement quality when indices disagree. Table 75-1 prints strict inequalities at these boundaries (p1150, ACC/AHA valve guideline).":["u6"],"copy|Classic triad once symptomatic: angina (50% 5-yr survival untreated), syncope (50% 3-yr mortality), heart failure (50% mortality at 2 yr — worst prognosis) (p1151).":["u7"],"copy|Sudden death <1% while truly asymptomatic (p1150); acquired platelet/von Willebrand dysfunction from severe AS causes epistaxis or ecchymoses in 20% of patients, and patients can also develop Heyde syndrome (GI bleeding from colonic angiodysplasias); these abnormalities resolve with valve replacement (p1151).":["u8"],"copy|Exercise testing in \"asymptomatic\" severe AS unmasks symptoms in up to a third; positive test (symptoms, flat BP response, ST changes) → 19% 2-yr symptom-free survival vs 85% if negative (p1151).":["u9"],"copy|5-yr symptom-free probability even in severe AS averages only 50% (p1151).":["u10"],"copy|BAV: enlargement rarely > 1.0 cm², 10% complication rate, restenosis by 6mo–1yr, 1-yr mortality 35–50% — bridge/palliation only, not a replacement substitute (p1152).":["u11"],"copy|SAVR bioprosthetic structural deterioration ~10% at 15–20 yr; Medicare operative mortality 8.8% overall, 6.0% at high-volume centers (p1152).":["u12"],"copy|Referral bias: 41% of >70y (European study) and 33% of >75y (second study: 1200 patients, 92 centers, 25 countries) with severe symptomatic AS were not offered surgery/valve replacement (p1152).":["u13"],"copy|The book's prohibitive surgical risk includes predicted one-year death or major morbidity over 50%, together with comorbidities or serious irreversible conditions (p1153). Later randomized NOTION follow-up reported valve outcomes through 10 years in lower-risk patients. Lifetime durability remains uncertain, especially in younger candidates.":["u14"],"copy|Table 75-3 (SAVR vs TAVR vs palliative selection): SAVR favored for longer life expectancy, few frailty markers, TAVR-unsuitable anatomy, or concomitant coronary artery disease or valve disease (lower reintervention/PPM/vascular-complication risk, longer stay); TAVR favored for shorter life expectancy, severe comorbidities/mobility issues, or prior chest irradiation (shorter stay, but accepts possible risk of repeat intervention and higher PPM/vascular-complication risk) (p1153).":["u15"],"copy|Palliative care (same table): symptom relief without life prolongation, avoiding periprocedural stroke/PPM/vascular-complication risk — for limited life expectancy, severe dementia/frailty/multiorgan impairment, valve anatomy/access unsuitable for TAVR, or goals of care against futile procedures (p1153).":["u16"],"copy|Chronic AI in 20–30% of >65y (p1153).":["u17"],"copy|Table 75-4 eponymous AI signs (owner-highlighted): Austin Flint murmur = low-pitched mid-diastolic rumble; Duroziez sign = to-and-fro femoral artery murmur with compression; Quincke sign = pulsatile nail beds; Watson's water-hammer pulse = bounding peripheral pulse; Corrigan pulse = rapidly rising/falling pulse on palpation (p1154).":["u18"],"copy|Asymptomatic, normal LV function: 0.2% incidence of sudden death, 3.5%/yr progress to symptoms, 6%/yr develop LV dysfunction or symptoms; once LV dysfunction develops, >25%/yr progress to symptomatic disease (p1153).":["u19"],"copy|Symptomatic mortality >10%/yr for angina, >20%/yr for heart failure (medical management only) (p1154).":["u20"],"copy|NYHA III–IV: 25%/yr mortality; NYHA II: 6%/yr (p1153).":["u21"],"copy|LVESD > 50 mm: 19%/yr develop dysfunction/symptoms/death; LVESD 40–50mm: 6%/yr (p1153).":["u22"],"copy|Table 75-5 severe cutoffs: jet-width/LVOT ratio ≥ 65%, vena contracta ≥ 0.6 cm, regurgitant volume ≥ 60 mL/beat, EROA ≥ 30 mm² (p1154).":["u23"],"copy|Severe chronic AR: symptoms are an indication for surgery regardless of EF when intervention is appropriate. In asymptomatic patients, the 2020 ACC/AHA threshold for LV systolic dysfunction is LVEF ≤55% without another cause. LVESD >50 mm or indexed LVESD >25 mm/m², and progressive LVEDD >65 mm in selected low-risk patients, represent distinct pathways. Severe AR at other cardiac surgery is an additional indication. Table 75-6 prints an older EF threshold of <50% and lists moderate AR during other cardiac surgery as a consideration (p1155).Current practice (not the book): the 65-mm AR threshold is LV end-diastolic dimension (LVEDD >65 mm, progressive, low surgical risk: may be considered); LVESD >50 mm is the end-systolic trigger — ACC/AHA valvular guideline, 2020.":["u24"],"copy|Severe LV dysfunction: operative mortality at least 10%, with lower postsurgical survival (p1155).":["u25"],"copy|Overwhelming majority rheumatic in etiology; normal MVA 4.0–5.0 cm², shrinks 0.09–0.32 cm²/yr; symptoms usually appear once area falls to 2.5–1.5 cm² (p1156).":["u26"],"copy|Symptom onset lags rheumatic fever by 20–40 years (p1156).":["u27"],"copy|Once symptomatic, 10-yr survival only 50–60%; asymptomatic 10-yr survival 80%; unoperated despite meeting surgical criteria: 10-yr survival < 30% (p1156).":["u28"],"copy|Rheumatic mitral stenosis: current ACC/AHA staging defines severe disease at MVA ≤1.5 cm² and very severe disease at ≤1.0 cm². Table 75-7's older echo category “severe” uses MVA <1.0 cm², PA pressure >50 mm Hg and mean gradient >10 mm Hg. Gradient varies with flow and heart rate (p1156).":["u29"],"copy|30–40% of older MS patients present with AF; with AF, 10-yr survival 25% vs 46% if they remain in sinus rhythm (p1156).":["u30"],"copy|Systemic emboli in 20% of patients (p1157).":["u31"],"copy|Percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty: 90% get symptomatic relief; freedom from complications/death 50–65% at 7 yr (up to 80–90% with favorable pre-procedure score) (p1157).":["u32"],"copy|Table 75-8 procedural risks: LV perforation 0.5–4.0%, systemic embolization 0.5–3.0%, MI 0.3–0.5%, death 1.0–2.0% (p1157).":["u33"],"copy|Surgical operative mortality up to 20% in older patients with comorbidities and pulmonary hypertension (p1158).":["u34"],"copy|~2% of the population; mitral valve prolapse (1–2.5% of population) is the most common cause (p1158).":["u35"],"copy|Severe chronic MR (especially with a flail leaflet): 7%/yr mortality (p1158).":["u36"],"copy|10-yr survival by EF: 32% if EF < 50%, 53% if borderline EF 50–60%, 70% if EF > 60% — all untreated (p1158).":["u37"],"copy|Table 75-10 severe cutoffs: vena contracta ≥ 0.7 cm, regurgitant volume ≥ 60 mL/beat, RF ≥ 50%; EROA row prints \"≥ 40\" cm² — almost certainly a misprint for ≥0.40 (see exam-trap note) (p1158).":["u38"],"copy|Mitral repair durability: p1159 describes good durability but prints 7–10% as “freedom from reoperation,” an inconsistent endpoint label. In a surgical cohort of 1,604 patients, actual freedom from reoperation after repair was 93.6% at 10 years. Durability depends on anatomy, repair technique and population.":["u39"],"copy|Operative mortality: 2% for repair vs 6% for replacement overall; a 292-patient >70y cohort found 0.7% in-hospital mortality for repair vs 14% for replacement (p1160).":["u40"],"copy|Table 75-12 risk criteria (combine per column, not PROM alone; organ counts = major organ dysfunction that will not improve postoperatively): low = STS-PROM < 4% and no frailty and no organ dysfunction and no procedure impediment; intermediate = 4–8% or 1 frailty index or 1 organ system or possible impediment; high = > 8%, ≥2 frailty indices, ≤2 organ systems, possible impediment; prohibitive = death/morbidity risk > 50% or ≥3 organ systems or severe impediment (p1160).":["u42"],"copy|Dental endocarditis prophylaxis in adults with eligible high-risk cardiac disease: amoxicillin 2 g orally once, 30–60 minutes before a procedure involving gingiva, periapical tissue or oral mucosa perforation. For penicillin allergy, options include cephalexin 2 g if appropriate, azithromycin/clarithromycin 500 mg or doxycycline 100 mg. Avoid cephalosporins after penicillin/ampicillin anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria. If oral medication is impossible, the AHA lists ampicillin 2 g IM/IV or cefazolin/ceftriaxone 1 g IM/IV where appropriate. Clindamycin in the historical table is no longer recommended for dental prophylaxis (p1161, AHA prevention wallet card).Current practice (not the book): clindamycin is no longer recommended for dental prophylaxis; penicillin-allergic options are cephalexin, azithromycin/clarithromycin or doxycycline PO, or cefazolin/ceftriaxone IM/IV — but no cephalosporin after anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria with penicillin/ampicillin — AHA endocarditis prophylaxis wallet card.":["u43"],"copy|Prosthetic valves: mechanical valves require VKA therapy, with INR target based on valve, position and thromboembolic risk (typically 2.5 for a current-generation mechanical aortic valve without added risk and 3.0 for mechanical mitral valves). US guidance considers VKA to target INR 2.5 for 3–6 months after selected surgical bioprosthetic aortic or mitral replacement. An independent indication may warrant ongoing anticoagulation. Table 75-15 prints an older routine aspirin combination and different bioprosthetic regimens (p1161).Current practice (not the book): with a mechanical valve on VKA, adding low-dose aspirin 75–100 mg/day for antiplatelet therapy requires a separate indication (ESC/EACTS: concomitant symptomatic atherosclerotic disease) and low bleeding risk; ESC/EACTS 2025 class IIa, level B — ESC/EACTS 2025, Eur Heart J 2025;46:4635; ACC/AHA 2020 class 2b, level B-R — ACC/AHA 2020, Circulation 2021;143:e142.":["u44"],"copy|Thromboembolism risk 1–2%/yr mechanical vs 0.7%/yr bioprosthetic, lower still for aortic- vs mitral-position prostheses (p1161).":["u45"],"copy|Supratherapeutic INR: assess bleeding, urgency and thrombotic risk. Without bleeding at INR 4.5–10, hold warfarin as appropriate and recheck INR. Routine vitamin K is generally unnecessary. The historical option of oral vitamin K 1–2.5 mg at INR 5–10 on p1161 is not a blanket treatment instruction (CHEST).Current practice (not the book): INR 4.5–10 with no bleeding: suggest against routine vitamin K; INR >10 without bleeding: oral vitamin K; major bleeding: 4-factor PCC plus vitamin K 5–10 mg slow IV — ACCP/CHEST 9th ed, 2012.":["u46"],"copy|When warfarin interruption is required for an elective procedure, CHEST generally advises stopping it at least five days beforehand. A bileaflet mechanical aortic valve without added risk generally does not require bridging. For higher-risk mechanical valves, weigh thrombotic and procedural bleeding risks with the valve team. CHEST 2022 suggests against routine bridging, while ACC/AHA allows it in selected high-risk patients. Restart when hemostasis permits (p1162 describes an older 48–72-hour approach).Current practice (not the book): stop warfarin ≥5 days (rather than <5 days) before an elective surgery/procedure (conditional recommendation) — ACCP/CHEST perioperative antithrombotic guideline, 2022.":["u47"],"copy|Bioprosthetic durability by age: 90% freedom from structural deterioration at 15–20 yr at age 70; 90–95% freedom from reoperation if age >75 (p1162).":["u48"],"copy|Aortic valve repair for calcific disease is rarely durable, but for normal leaflets it achieves 85% freedom from reoperation at 10 years (p1163).":["u49"],"copy|MS \"usually develop symptoms\" once MVA falls to 2.5 to 1.5 cm² (p1156), which overlaps Table 75-7's own mild cutoff (>1.5 cm²) — read literally, symptom onset and \"mild\" grade can coincide.":["u52"],"copy|In asymptomatic severe AR with preserved EF, distinguish LVESD >50 mm (or indexed LVESD >25 mm/m²) from progressive LVEDD >65 mm in a selected low-risk patient. The narrative on p1155 uses LVESD for 65 mm, while Table 75-6 leaves the dimension unspecified. The 2020 ACC/AHA guideline specifies end-diastolic dimension for the latter threshold.":["u53"],"recall|Recall":["u54"],"copy|Symptomatic patients with severe aortic stenosis":["u56"],"copy|Asymptomatic patients with severe aortic stenosis and left ventricular dysfunction (ejection fraction < 50%)":["u57"],"copy|Asymptomatic patients with severe aortic stenosis undergoing other cardiac surgery":["u58"],"copy|Symptomatic patients with low-flow/low-gradient severe aortic stenosis with left ventricular dysfunction":["u59"],"copy|Symptomatic patients with low-flow/low-gradient severe aortic stenosis and LVEF > 50%, if clinical, hemodynamic, and anatomic data support valve obstruction as the most likely cause of symptoms":["u60"],"copy|CHAPTER 75s / HAZZARD":["u61"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 75; the book and the full chapter are the reference; additional current-practice valve reading: ESC/EACTS 2025 and ACC/AHA 2020 with corrections.":["u62"],"copy|Aortic stenosis (AS)":["u63"],"copy|Aortic insufficiency (AI)":["u64"],"copy|Mitral stenosis (MS)":["u65"],"copy|Mitral regurgitation (MR)":["u66"],"copy|Perioperative risk, anticoagulation, prosthetic valves":["u67"],"copy|1.":["u69"],"copy|In patients older than 65, what fraction of aortic stenosis is caused by calcific degeneration of a trileaflet valve, and at what age does a bicuspid valve's stenosis typically present?":["u70"],"copy|90% of AS in patients over 65 is calcific degeneration of a tricuspid valve; bicuspid-valve stenosis usually presents earlier, in the fourth to sixth decade (p1149).":["u71"],"copy|2.":["u72"],"copy|Name the classic triad of symptoms once aortic stenosis becomes symptomatic, and rank them by average survival without valve replacement.":["u73"],"copy|Angina (50% 5-yr survival), syncope (50% 3-yr mortality), and heart failure (~50% mortality at 2 years — the worst prognosis of the three) (p1151).":["u74"],"copy|3.":["u75"],"copy|On exercise testing, what result predicts a poor 2-year symptom-free survival in \"asymptomatic\" severe aortic stenosis, and what are the two survival rates?":["u76"],"copy|Development of symptoms, failure to generate a 20 mmHg increase in blood pressure, or ST-segment abnormalities predicts a 19% 2-year symptom-free survival, versus 85% if none of these occur (p1151).":["u77"],"copy|4.":["u78"],"copy|What STS risk cutoff defines \"prohibitive\" surgical risk for TAVR consideration, and what is the durability caveat for TAVR valves?":["u79"],"copy|The historical definition was a predicted one-year risk of death or major morbidity above 50%, taking irreversible comorbidity into account (p1153).":["u80"],"copy|Randomized NOTION follow-up now extends to ten years.":["u80"],"copy|Very-long-term durability and the feasibility of later valve-in-valve intervention remain patient-specific.":["u80"],"copy|6.":["u81"],"copy|What is the annual incidence of LV dysfunction or symptoms in asymptomatic aortic insufficiency with an end-systolic dimension greater than 50 mm, versus 40–50 mm?":["u82"],"copy|19%/year if LVESD > 50 mm, versus 6%/year if LVESD is 40–50 mm (p1153).":["u83"],"copy|7.":["u84"],"copy|In asymptomatic severe aortic regurgitation with preserved LV function, how do LV end-systolic and progressive end-diastolic enlargement affect referral for surgery?":["u85"],"copy|LVESD >50 mm or indexed LVESD >25 mm/m² can support surgery.":["u86"],"copy|Progressive enlargement into the severe LV end-diastolic range above 65 mm can support consideration in selected low-risk patients.":["u86"],"copy|Page 1155 labels this latter dimension differently from ACC/AHA 2020.":["u86"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): the 65-mm AR threshold is LV end-diastolic dimension (LVEDD >65 mm, progressive, low surgical risk: may be considered); LVESD >50 mm is the end-systolic trigger — ACC/AHA valvular guideline, 2020.":["u87"],"copy|8.":["u88"],"copy|What mitral valve area range typically corresponds to symptom onset in mitral stenosis, and what is the normal MVA?":["u89"],"copy|Symptoms usually develop once the valve area falls to 2.5–1.5 cm²; normal MVA is 4.0–5.0 cm² (p1156).":["u90"],"copy|9.":["u91"],"copy|In mitral stenosis, how does 10-year survival compare between patients who remain in sinus rhythm and those who develop atrial fibrillation?":["u92"],"copy|46% 10-year survival in sinus rhythm versus 25% with atrial fibrillation (p1156).":["u93"],"copy|10.":["u94"],"copy|What echocardiographic scoring system selects patients for percutaneous mitral balloon valvuloplasty, and what features give the most favorable (lowest) score?":["u95"],"copy|The Wilkins Score; the lowest (most favorable) scores go to valves with the greatest leaflet mobility, least subvalvular thickening, most normal leaflet thickness, and least calcification (p1157).":["u96"],"copy|11.":["u97"],"copy|What is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation, and roughly what fraction of the population does it affect?":["u98"],"copy|Mitral valve prolapse, present in 1% to 2.5% of the population (p1158).":["u99"],"copy|12.":["u100"],"copy|Compare operative mortality for mitral valve repair versus replacement, both in the overall population and in a cohort of patients older than 70.":["u101"],"copy|Overall: 2% (repair) vs 6% (replacement).":["u102"],"copy|In a 292-patient cohort older than 70: 0.7% (repair) vs 14% (replacement) in-hospital mortality (p1160).":["u103"],"copy|13.":["u104"],"copy|What INR range and duration of anticoagulation apply to a bioprosthetic aortic valve without additional risk factors?":["u105"],"copy|If VKA is chosen after surgical bioprosthetic aortic valve replacement, target INR 2.5 (usual range 2–3).":["u106"],"copy|ACC/AHA considers 3–6 months reasonable in selected patients at low bleeding risk.":["u106"],"copy|Mechanical prostheses require lifelong VKA, and another indication can prolong anticoagulation with a bioprosthesis (p1161).":["u106"],"copy|14.":["u107"],"copy|Before elective surgery, how long can warfarin be held preoperatively in a patient with a mechanical aortic valve and no risk factors, and when is it restarted — with or without heparin bridging?":["u108"],"copy|For elective procedures requiring interruption, stop warfarin at least five days beforehand and verify adequate INR decline.":["u109"],"copy|A bileaflet mechanical aortic valve without added thromboembolic risk generally needs no heparin bridge.":["u109"],"copy|Resume warfarin once hemostasis permits, often within 12–24 hours (p1162, CHEST 2022).":["u109"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): stop warfarin ≥5 days (rather than <5 days) before an elective surgery/procedure (conditional recommendation) — ACCP/CHEST perioperative antithrombotic guideline, 2022.":["u110"],"copy|15.":["u111"],"copy|What is the standard oral antibiotic and dose for endocarditis prophylaxis in a penicillin-allergic patient able to take oral medication?":["u112"],"copy|For eligible adults with penicillin allergy, an oral AHA alternative is cephalexin 2 g if there was no anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria, or azithromycin/clarithromycin 500 mg or doxycycline 100 mg.":["u113"],"copy|Give one dose 30–60 minutes before the indicated dental procedure.":["u113"],"copy|The clindamycin regimen printed on p1161 is historical.":["u113"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): clindamycin is no longer recommended for dental prophylaxis; penicillin-allergic options are cephalexin, azithromycin/clarithromycin or doxycycline PO — but no cephalosporin after anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria with penicillin/ampicillin — AHA endocarditis prophylaxis wallet card.":["u114"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, p.1152 · Read source":["u115"],"copy|Indications for valve replacement in aortic stenosis":["u116"],"copy|Answer":["u118","u119","u120","u121","u122","u123","u124","u125","u126","u127","u128","u129","u130","u131"],"copy|What annual rate of LV dysfunction, symptoms or death was reported in asymptomatic aortic insufficiency with an end-systolic dimension greater than 50 mm, versus 40–50 mm?":["u82"],"copy|Mechanical aortic valve without thromboembolic risk factors: the book gives a 48–72-hour preoperative warfarin hold, restart within 24 hours after surgery, and no heparin bridge. It describes bridging for a mechanical mitral valve or a mechanical aortic valve with high-risk features (p1162).Current practice (not the book): CHEST suggests a warfarin hold of at least five days when interruption is necessary and resumption within 24 hours when hemostasis permits. CHEST suggests against routine bridging. ACC/AHA allows individualized bridging for higher-risk mechanical valves and advises interruption without bridging for a bileaflet mechanical aortic valve without additional thromboembolic risk. CHEST 2022 · ACC/AHA 2020.":["u47"],"copy|Hazzard gives a 48–72-hour preoperative hold for a mechanical aortic valve without thromboembolic risk factors.":["u109"],"copy|Restart warfarin within 24 hours after surgery.":["u109"],"copy|In this setting the book specifies no bridging heparin (p1162).":["u280"],"copy|Which oral regimens does Table 75-14 give for dental endocarditis prophylaxis in an eligible patient with penicillin allergy?":["u112"],"copy|The printed table lists clindamycin 600 mg PO or cephalexin 2 g PO, given once 1 hour before the procedure (Table 75-14, p1161).":["u281"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): AHA dental guidance removes clindamycin.":["u282"],"copy|For eligible adults with penicillin allergy, give cephalexin 2 g, azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg, or doxycycline 100 mg PO 30–60 minutes before the procedure.":["u113"],"copy|Avoid cephalosporins after anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria with penicillin or ampicillin.":["u283"],"copy|AHA prevention card, dental regimens based on the 2021 scientific statement.":["u284"],"copy|Severe AS (Table 75-1, p1150): aortic valve area <1.0 cm², jet velocity >4 m/s, mean gradient >40 mm Hg.Current practice (not the book): ACC/AHA describes severe high-gradient AS as peak velocity ≥4.0 m/s or mean gradient ≥40 mm Hg; valve area is typically ≤1.0 cm² — ACC/AHA 2020.":["u6"],"copy|Hazzard defines prohibitive SAVR risk as an STS predicted risk of morbidity and mortality over 50% due to comorbidity or serious irreversible conditions. It describes TAVR durability beyond five years as unknown and notes the possibility of valve-in-valve reintervention (p1153).Current practice (not the book): randomized NOTION follow-up reports valve outcomes through 10 years in lower-risk patients — NOTION follow-up, 2024.":["u14"],"copy|Severe chronic AR (Table 75-6, p1155): valve replacement is indicated for symptomatic severe AR regardless of LV function, asymptomatic severe AR with EF <50%, or severe AR during other cardiac surgery. Consider surgery with normal LV function and LVESD >50 mm, or for moderate AR during other cardiac surgery. Progressive LV dilatation >65 mm with low surgical risk is a possible indication.Current practice (not the book): ACC/AHA uses LVEF ≤55% for stage C2 severe AR and distinguishes LVESD >50 mm or indexed LVESD >25 mm/m² from progressive severe LV end-diastolic enlargement; the 65-mm AR threshold is LV end-diastolic dimension (LVEDD >65 mm, progressive, low surgical risk: may be considered); LVESD >50 mm is the end-systolic trigger — ACC/AHA valvular guideline, 2020.":["u24"],"copy|Severe mitral stenosis in Table 75-7 (p1156): mitral valve area <1.0 cm², pulmonary artery pressure >50 mm Hg and mean gradient >10 mm Hg. Gradient varies with flow and heart rate.Current practice (not the book): ACC/AHA staging defines severe rheumatic MS by mitral valve area ≤1.5 cm² and very severe MS by ≤1.0 cm² — ACC/AHA 2020.":["u29"],"copy|Mitral repair durability: p1159 describes good durability but prints 7–10% as “freedom from reoperation,” an inconsistent endpoint label.Current practice (not the book): in a surgical cohort of 1,604 patients, actual freedom from reoperation after repair was 93.6% at 10 years. Durability depends on anatomy, repair technique and population — Glauber et al., 2015.":["u39"],"copy|Endocarditis prophylaxis for eligible adults (Table 75-14, p1161): give amoxicillin 2 g PO 1 hour before. For penicillin allergy the table lists clindamycin 600 mg or cephalexin 2 g PO 1 hour before. If oral dosing is impossible, the table lists ampicillin 2 g IV/IM 30 minutes before, or clindamycin 600 mg IV or cefazolin 1 g IV/IM 30 minutes before for penicillin allergy.Current practice (not the book): clindamycin is no longer recommended for dental prophylaxis; penicillin-allergic options are cephalexin, azithromycin/clarithromycin or doxycycline PO, or cefazolin/ceftriaxone IM/IV — but no cephalosporin after anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria with penicillin/ampicillin — AHA endocarditis prophylaxis wallet card.":["u43"],"copy|Prosthetic valves (Table 75-15, p1161): a mechanical aortic valve uses INR 2–3, or 2.5–3.5 with additional risk factors; a mechanical mitral valve uses INR 2.5–3.5. The table adds aspirin 75–325 mg/day for mechanical valves. For a bioprosthetic aortic or mitral valve without additional risk factors, it gives INR 2–3 for 3 months plus aspirin 75–100 mg/day. With risk factors the printed bioprosthetic ranges are 2–3 for aortic and 2.5–3.5 for mitral, with aspirin 75–100 mg/day.Current practice (not the book): US guidance targets INR 2.5 for a current-generation mechanical aortic valve without additional thromboembolic risk and 3.0 for a mechanical mitral valve; VKA to INR 2.5 for 3–6 months is reasonable after selected surgical bioprosthetic replacements. With a mechanical valve on VKA, adding low-dose aspirin 75–100 mg/day for antiplatelet therapy requires a separate indication (ESC/EACTS: concomitant symptomatic atherosclerotic disease) and low bleeding risk; ESC/EACTS 2025 class IIa, level B — ESC/EACTS 2025, Eur Heart J 2025;46:4635; ACC/AHA 2020 class 2b, level B-R — ACC/AHA 2020, Circulation 2021;143:e142.":["u44"],"copy|Supratherapeutic INR (p1161): Hazzard states that INR 5–10 can be treated by holding warfarin and giving oral vitamin K 1–2.5 mg. Monitor INR daily until it falls below 5.Current practice (not the book): INR 4.5–10 with no bleeding: suggest against routine vitamin K; INR >10 without bleeding: oral vitamin K; major bleeding: 4-factor PCC plus vitamin K 5–10 mg slow IV — ACCP/CHEST 9th ed, 2012.":["u46"],"copy|With asymptomatic severe AR and preserved LV function, Table 75-6 lists LVESD >50 mm for consideration of surgery. For low-risk surgery the table describes progressive LV dilatation >65 mm without specifying the dimension, while the narrative specifically calls it LVESD (p1155). See the current-practice comparison.":["u53"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): under 2025 European guidance, early replacement should also be considered alongside close surveillance when severe high-gradient stenosis is genuinely asymptomatic (normal exercise test if feasible), EF is at least 50%, and procedural risk is low.":["u184"],"copy|Table 75-14 lists clindamycin 600 mg or cephalexin 2 g orally 1 hour before the procedure for a penicillin-allergic adult (p1161).":["u285"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): AHA dental guidance instead includes azithromycin 500 mg 30–60 minutes before the procedure and removes clindamycin.":["u236"],"copy|Hazzard defines prohibitive SAVR risk as STS predicted morbidity and mortality >50% due to comorbidity or serious irreversible conditions.":["u80"],"copy|It describes TAVR valve durability beyond five years as unknown (p1153).":["u286"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): the NOTION randomized trial subsequently reported 10-year outcomes in lower-risk patients — NOTION follow-up, 2024.":["u287"],"copy|In asymptomatic severe aortic regurgitation with preserved LV function, how do LV end-systolic and progressive LV enlargement affect referral for surgery?":["u85"],"copy|Table 75-6 lists LVESD >50 mm as a reason to consider surgery and progressive LV dilatation >65 mm as a possible indication when surgical risk is low.":["u288"],"copy|The table leaves the latter dimension unspecified; the accompanying prose calls it LVESD (p1155).":["u86"],"copy|Table 75-15 gives INR 2–3 for the first 3 months plus aspirin 75–100 mg daily after bioprosthetic aortic valve replacement without additional risk factors.":["u106"],"copy|For a mechanical aortic valve it gives INR 2–3, increasing to 2.5–3.5 with thromboembolic risk factors, plus aspirin 75–325 mg daily (p1161).":["u289"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ACC/AHA considers VKA to a target INR of 2.5 for 3–6 months reasonable after selected surgical bioprosthetic aortic or mitral replacement — ACC/AHA 2020.":["u290"],"copy|CHAPTER 75 / HAZZARD":["u61"],"copy|Table 75-10's severe regurgitant-orifice-area cutoff for MR prints \"≥ 40\" cm²; the row's other two columns (<0.20, 0.20–0.39) make ≥ 0.40 the obviously intended value (p1158).":["u50"],"table-75-2|Table 75-2":["u55"],"copy|Exam traps":["u68"],"copy|Elsewhere in the same chapter, repair-vs-replacement mortality is instead quoted as 1–2% vs 5.4–6.4% (p1163) — a second, different pair of numbers for the same comparison; the two estimates differ.":["u41"],"copy|Mitral repair-vs-replacement operative mortality is given twice with different numbers: 2% vs 6% overall / 0.7% vs 14% in a >70y cohort (p1160), and later 1–2% vs 5.4–6.4% (p1163).":["u51"],"copy|June 2023 Q97":["u117"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[291,-159],[343,-158],[393,-156],[436,0]]}
